<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Tier 4 — WASM host. The ONLY C# in the repository.

    It exists for one mechanical reason: the WebAssembly JS-interop
    attributes are consumed by a Roslyn source generator, so [JSExport] is
    inert in an F# assembly — verified by publishing an F# module with
    [<JSExport>] and probing it in Chromium, where getAssemblyExports
    returned an empty object.

    It must stay pure marshalling: every method forwards a string to the F#
    kernel and returns a string, so it cannot inspect or decide anything
    about a command (TE-R-091). If a method here ever needs to know what a
    command means, the boundary has been breached.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>browser-wasm</RuntimeIdentifier>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <WasmMainJSPath>main.js</WasmMainJSPath>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <!--
      Trimming is off deliberately, not as a convenience, and the reason has
      changed — it is worth recording accurately rather than leaving the old
      note in place.

      It WAS reflection-based System.Text.Json in TimeEntry.Persistence
      (IL2026). That is fixed: `Serialization` is now written by hand against
      Utf8JsonWriter and JsonDocument, and IL2026 is gone.

      What blocks trimming now is FSharp.Core's `%A` formatting.
      `sformat.fs` calls Type.InvokeMember and `printf.fs` calls
      MakeGenericMethod, neither of which the trimmer can analyse (IL2080,
      IL2070, IL2060). Suppressing those and trimming anyway would risk
      degrading `sprintf "%A"` at run time — which is how every domain
      rejection currently reaches the user, so it would fail as unreadable
      error messages in the browser and nowhere else.

      WI-0030's original premise was also wrong and is corrected in
      Serialization's own header: System.Text.Json's source generator is a
      Roslyn generator, and F# does not run Roslyn generators.

      The real fix is to stop using `%A` for user-visible text and word each
      rejection explicitly — which TE-R-053 arguably requires anyway, since
      `CatalogueRejected (ProjectIsArchived ProjectId "retired-client")` is
      exactly the technical vocabulary that requirement forbids. WI-0047
      tracks it. Until then this stays false rather than suppressed.
    -->
    <PublishTrimmed>false</PublishTrimmed>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Served alongside the app bundle. styles.css is the adopted design
         system from static-ui-screens (DF-TE-0008), linked rather than
         copied so the design authority stays in one place. -->
    <WasmExtraFilesToDeploy Include="index.html" />
    <WasmExtraFilesToDeploy Include="sample-entry.json" />
    <WasmExtraFilesToDeploy Include="sample-entry-2.json" />
    <WasmExtraFilesToDeploy Include="sample-entry-3.json" />
    <WasmExtraFilesToDeploy Include="sample-catalogue.json" />
    <WasmExtraFilesToDeploy Include="sample-versions.json" />
    <WasmExtraFilesToDeploy Include="../../static-ui-screens/styles.css" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../TimeEntry.Kernel/TimeEntry.Kernel.fsproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
